1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Someone asks, what gradient to set up
the Mises would you recommend?

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.400 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
I would say usually, Adam or gradient
descent with momentum are pretty good,

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.279 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
and a lot of the time you should
consider using gradient clipping.

4
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.559 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
The next part of our overall roadmap
is to discuss what happens when we have rounding error,

5
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:18.960 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
when we have underflow, overflow,

6
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.240 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
all of these issues that happen when
we use a finite number of bits to represent real numbers.

7
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.320 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
This is really important.

8
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.480 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
I know it sounds boring to talk about
this low-level stuff with bits,

9
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.480 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but writing numerically
precise code is actually a deep learning super skill.

10
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.600 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
A lot of the time you'll
find that deep learning algorithms sort of work when you code them up.

11
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.480 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
The loss will go down, the
accuracy will go up,

12
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.760 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
the accuracy will get
within a few percentage points of the state of the art,

13
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.880 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and you can check your code
really carefully, and you can find that there aren't really any bugs,

14
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.560 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but maybe there are some
things that aren't quite optimal about it.

15
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.800 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
That's often caused by a
loss of numerical precision,

16
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.800 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
where you run your gradient
descent algorithm,

17
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.280 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and you don't get quite of
good as results as you think you should.

18
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.719 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Another time you'll find
that your deep learning algorithm explodes,

19
00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:10.320 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
you get a lot of
not-in-number values,

20
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.320 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
you get a lot of very large
values,

21
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.200 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and this might happen even
if you make your learning rate relatively small,

22
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.640 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and you think everything
should be stable.

23
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:22.640 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Both of these problems
actually usually come from writing code that doesn't have high enough

24
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.680 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
numerical precision.

25
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.359 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So being able to write
numerically precise code can really differentiate how well you perform

26
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.280 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
as a deep learning
practitioner.

27
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.200 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
A lot of people have heard
the story of how when I first thought of generative adversarial nets,

28
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.400 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
I was at a bar, and I went
home, and I coded them up immediately.

29
00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:42.240 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And within an hour or so
later, I actually had them working.

30
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.359 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
A lot of people have
wondered how I was able to do that,

31
00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:51.439 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and part of the answer is
that I knew about all of this really boring numerical precision stuff.

32
00:01:51.519 --> 00:01:54.640 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So I didn't waste any time
shooting myself in the foot,

33
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.719 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
writing numerically and
precise code,

34
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.560 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and that reduces the amount
of time it takes to get the algorithm working a lot.

35
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.319 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
The basic idea is that when
you use a finite number of bits to represent a real number,

36
00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:11.680 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
you get both rounding
errors and what are called truncation errors.

37
00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:15.919 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
In a digital computer,
we're usually talking about using float32,

38
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:19.840 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
or some other similar
schemes to represent real numbers.

39
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.039 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
That's especially true when
we're using CUDA on GPUs.

40
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.080 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Sometimes we use other
floating formats. A lot of the problems I'm going to describe here

41
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.560 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
will often mostly go away
if you're able to switch to float64,

42
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.599 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but that usually requires
slower computation on GPU,

43
00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:41.439 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
or may even force you to
move to CPU. It will also force you to use more memory.

44
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.840 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So if you can write
numerically precise code using float32,

45
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.439 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
then that can be a big
advantage.

46
00:02:47.439 --> 00:02:56.639 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
There's basically three
categories of problems that happen on these kinds of 32-bit representations.

47
00:02:56.639 --> 00:02:59.120 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So first, most numbers that
you work with,

48
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.840 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
they're not going to cause
catastrophic problems on their own,

49
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.520 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but they are going to get
rounded.

50
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.840 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
If you have some real
number x and you turn it into a representation on computer,

51
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.879 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
it's going to get rounded
to some value x plus delta,

52
00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:15.520 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
where delta is just some
small value describing the rounding error that happened,

53
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.159 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
that you'll be off by a
little bit from what you meant to be.

54
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.400 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
A larger problem is when
you have some extremely large value of x,

55
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.760 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
you just can't represent it
with the range of numbers that your float32 values can represent.

56
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And it will actually get
replaced by some placeholder, like,

57
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.200 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
inf, just saying that x is
too large to represent.

58
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.640 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And so now it's not really
a finite value anymore.

59
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.640 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
You've got this infinite
number,

60
00:03:42.719 --> 00:03:48.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
and your code will probably
still go ahead and keep doing arithmetic on this number as if it

61
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
were a real value, and then
you'll get in trouble.

62
00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:55.359 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
There's another topic which
is still basically a kind of rounding error,

63
00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:58.079 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but it's a particularly
harmful kind of rounding error.

64
00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:02.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
If you have a very small
value of x, it can get replaced by zero.

65
00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:07.119 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
This is especially harmful
because a lot of the algorithms in deep learning often assume that

66
00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:11.199 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
we have some very small but
non-zero variable.

67
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.119 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Like, for example, if you
use the logistic sigmoid activation function,

68
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.039 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
you can pass very negative
values to the logistic sigmoid.

69
00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:23.199 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And algebraically, they'll
never become quite zero,

70
00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:25.839 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but in a computer they can
definitely get rounded down to zero.

71
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.360 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And so people are asking,
aren't these numerical issues already addressed in the current

72
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.120 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
deep learning libraries? And the
answer is basically, yes, but you need to know when to look out

73
00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.879 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
for them. A lot of the time you write
your own code, and you might end up rewriting a version

74
00:04:42.879 --> 00:04:48.480 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
of something that already has an
numerically stable variant. Sometimes you might need to,

75
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.280 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
for example, compute a softmax over
several tensors, instead of just one tensor,

76
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.280 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
and you'll need to know how to do that
in an numerically stable way if your library only

77
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:05.920 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
provides a softmax for a single
tensor. Some other people are asking if there's any pointers on

78
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.879 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
the best way to deal with nands.
That's basically what the next several slides will do.

79
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.960 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Someone asked, how does the 64-bit
representation solve the nand error?

80
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.439 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Well, the basic idea is that if you
have 32 bits, there's a certain range of values that you can

81
00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:27.920 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
represent. If you have 64 bits, that
range expands. So a program that might cause a nand when you

82
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:34.319 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
use 32 bits might actually avoid the
nand when you use 64 bits. If you use a program that uses

83
00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:38.399 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
even bigger numbers, you can
eventually still get nands for 64 bits. It's just that

84
00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.319 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
fewer programs cause problems using 64
bits than using 32. One example is if you look at the

85
00:05:46.319 --> 00:05:52.480 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Pylon 2 repository that I used to work
on in grad school before we had tensor flow, you can look

86
00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.680 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
at some of the unit tests, and when I
compute the k-old divergence between two Gaussian distributions,

87
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:04.720 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
if I use float 64, the unit test
expects the k-old divergence to have a minimum value of 0.

88
00:06:06.079 --> 00:06:13.360 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
But if I use float 32, the unit test
actually has to allow the k-old divergence to go slightly negative.

89
00:06:14.399 --> 00:06:19.920 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So one example of how we can get
rounding errors and they can affect this code is what happens when

90
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.439 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
we add a very small number to a larger
number. So we're going to make an array using 32-bit floats,

91
00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:31.360 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
and we're going to store the value 0
and the value 10 to the minus 8 in this array. If we compute

92
00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:39.279 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
the argmax of this array, the argmax
is 1 because position 0 has a value of 0, position 1 has a

93
00:06:39.279 --> 00:06:44.720 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
positive but very small value of 10 to
the minus 8. So now suppose that we take these numbers and

94
00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:51.279 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
we add them to a much larger number,
we add them to 1. Well, 0 is going to have 1 added to it and

95
00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:58.959 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
end up with a value of 1. Ideally, 1
plus 10 to the minus 8 would be represented as 1, but

96
00:06:59.679 --> 00:07:05.679 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
it's at 1 point, you know, 0, 0, 0, 0,
0, 1 after the correct number of 0s. In this case, it's

97
00:07:05.679 --> 00:07:10.719 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
actually going to get rounded to 1,
which isn't quite what we want. When you take the argmax of

98
00:07:10.719 --> 00:07:16.559 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
the newer array where we added 1 to
it, we actually get that the argmax is 0 now because NumPy breaks ties

99
00:07:17.519 --> 00:07:24.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
to the first index that's tied for the
max. So this addition of a number that caused rounding

100
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.280 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
error has actually downstream changed
the results quite a lot. We only really wanted to extract a

101
00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.920 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
single bit from this computation and
we're now actually getting the value of that bit wrong.

102
00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.199 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
There's some secondary effects that
happen when we have rounding error and overflow and

103
00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:46.079 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
underflow errors. What happens is
after the first error, we do some more math and then the second

104
00:07:46.079 --> 00:07:52.159 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
error causes a bigger problem. So for
example, so we have some code that computes x minus y.

105
00:07:52.879 --> 00:07:58.319 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
x minus y is a relatively safe thing
to do to start with. There's definitely some ways that

106
00:07:58.319 --> 00:08:02.800 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
can go wrong, but there's not anything
fundamentally wrong with doing a subtraction. But now suppose

107
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:09.759 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
that x overflows to nth, y overflows
to nth, well then we've got infinity minus infinity and we

108
00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:15.039 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
don't really know which infinity is
larger. This kind of operation is not really defined. So now

109
00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:21.599 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
our x minus y will turn these nth into
nth. A lot of the time when you have overflow, the way that

110
00:08:21.599 --> 00:08:27.439 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
you first see it is that you'll get a
nth in your loss function or your gradient. So somewhere in

111
00:08:27.439 --> 00:08:32.879 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
your computational graph, you'll get
an nth and then by the time it actually gets printed out or

112
00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:37.120 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
plotted somewhere, it will have been
turned into a nth by these additional steps of arithmetic that

113
00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:43.679 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
came afterward. One of the main
functions that's often the culprit of numerical precision problems

114
00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:52.879 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
in deep learning programs is the x
function that computes the natural logarithms base e raised to

115
00:08:52.879 --> 00:08:58.320 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
the power of x. This will overflow for
large values of x. And a lot of the time when I say large

116
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:04.080 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
values, I am talking about extremely
large values. For x, when I say large values, I'm actually talking

117
00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:11.280 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
about numbers that are not very big.
If you're using float32, a value of 89 past 2x will overflow to n.

118
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
To make sure that your program doesn't
have trouble with x, just never use large x as the argument

119
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.480 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
for x. And make sure that you design
the structure of your functions that you compute so that they will

120
00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.800 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
never actually pass a large x to x. A
lot of the rest of this lecture will be about ideas for how you

121
00:09:30.799 --> 00:09:38.240 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
can prevent that from happening in
specific cases. The other big problem with x is that if your

122
00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.559 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
argument to it is very negative, x of
x can underflow. This may not be a problem. If you think about it,

123
00:09:45.599 --> 00:09:51.599 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
x of a very negative number is just
some small number epsilon. And if you round that number down to

124
00:09:51.599 --> 00:09:57.039 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
zero, you've only made a mistake of
size epsilon. So if that was your final output of the function,

125
00:09:57.039 --> 00:10:02.559 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
it's not a very big error.
Unfortunately, if you then do other math on top of this output,

126
00:10:03.279 --> 00:10:08.799 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
rounding a small number epsilon down
to zero can be catastrophic if you're using x of x in a

127
00:10:08.799 --> 00:10:13.519 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
denominator or as an argument to a
logarithm. And so on, it can cause really big problems.

128
00:10:15.599 --> 00:10:21.679 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Another case where you can sometimes
encounter danger is when you have subtraction, especially if

129
00:10:21.679 --> 00:10:28.639 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
you want to guarantee that
the output of the subtraction is going to be positive. So if you have two

130
00:10:28.639 --> 00:10:33.919 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
values x and y, and you
know algebraically that you can prove that x is always greater than y,

131
00:10:34.639 --> 00:10:39.679 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
in a computer, you may
actually find that x minus y ends up being negative. So if you rely it on

132
00:10:39.679 --> 00:10:45.599 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
x minus y being positive in
subsequent steps of your program, you can run into trouble. One case

133
00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.560 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
where you see this a lot is
computing the variance. So there's one way that you can write the

134
00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:56.399 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
variance where it's the
expectation of a squared value. If you implement the variance this way in

135
00:10:56.399 --> 00:11:02.560 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
your code, it's always
going to give you positive numbers because the output of the the squaring value

136
00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:10.160 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
will will always be non
negative. I shouldn't say positive, sorry. And then if you look at the

137
00:11:10.159 --> 00:11:17.360 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
difference between the
expectation of the squared value of the function and the square of the

138
00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:22.240 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
expected value of the
function, algebraically that's a perfectly valid way of rearranging this

139
